//   reason: "Self appointment",
//   status: "scheduled",
//   fee: 50,
// }));
// Result: MongoServerError: Document failed validation
// -> Every $jsonSchema rule is satisfied; only the $expr rule fails.

console.log("FINAL counts:", {
  users: db.users.countDocuments(),
  appointments: db.appointments.countDocuments(),
});

// Result: { users: 0, appointments: 0 } - both validated collections are empty and ready for Task 4.


// ============================================================
// WRITTEN ANSWERS
// ============================================================

// 1) Name two rules in this project that a validator cannot express.
//
// A validator only sees the one document being written, never other documents
// or collections. So it cannot check that doctorId points to a user that really
// exists and has role "doctor" (or that patientId points to a patient). It also
// cannot enforce that an email is unique across all users or that a doctor has
// no two overlapping appointments; those need a unique index or application logic.

// 2) What is the difference between strict and moderate, and when would you
//    start with moderate?
//
// With strict, every insert and every update is checked against the validator.
// With moderate, inserts and updates to documents that are already valid are
// checked, but documents that were invalid before the validator was added can
// still be updated without checks. I would start with moderate (often with warn)
// when adding a validator to a collection that already has old data, so the
// application keeps working while I find and fix the violators, and then switch
// to strict.


